all which reads persisted state. Wait: the second call passes index 1 and a different runRoot; state comes from readState(statePath). But wait — readState: `state.nextIndex !== index` check. After call 1 succeeded (code 0, timedOut false → nextState.nextIndex = 1). Good.\n\nBut spawn 1: index 1 command is `netscript init my-app --db postgres --service --yes` — non-cd → spawn. Good, spawns has 2 entries. But careful: in the second call, `entry.command.startsWith('aspire wait ')` — no. OK.\n\nAlso `assertEquals(spawns[1].env, spawns[0].env)` — both are the same object reference actually? No — second call recomputes env via readmeCommandEnvironment → a new object with same values. assertEquals does deep equality. Fine.\n\nAlso the receipt check: `receipts/01.json` in temporaryRoot — note the second call (index 1) writes receipts/02.json into `resolve(dirname(statePath), 'receipts')` = temporaryRoot/receipts/02.json. So receipts dir is under statePath's dirname (temporaryRoot), not runRoot. Fine. But note: the second call's statePath is the same, and receipts dir is NOT reset at index ≥1 (only at initializeState). Good.\n\nHmm, one subtle thing: the second call passes a different runRoot, but the persisted state's cwd = the first runRoot. The `cd` resolution etc. uses state.cwd. Not an issue for the test.\n\nNow Tier A item 2: \"later indexes read the root from persisted state, not re-derive it\" — YES: readme-command.ts:92 `readmeCommandEnvironment(state.denoInstallRoot)` where state comes from readState (line 71). The env PATH/ DENO_INSTALL_ROOT are recomputed from the persisted root (the PATH ambient tail is fresh from the walker process env — appropriate). PASS.\n\nItem 1: initializeState removes pre-existing `<runRoot>/.deno-install` recursive, only NotFound tolerated, then recreates, at index 0 only (initializeState called only when index===0, line 69-70). PASS. Note: also called before receipts removal — ordering: deno-install removal happens first, fine.\n\nWait