ture dropped env entirely). So the fix (isolation) is entirely in 0650f6f7b. I'll verify the test fails at b1aafaaa6 by a revert-run locally (safe — fake spawn).\n\nItem 7: gates. Run them.\n\nItem 8: other callers of runAspireCommand: grep showed only aspire-walk.ts internal (requireAspireSuccess calls run(command, cwd, timeoutMs) — 3 args, env optional, fine) and readme-command.ts. Plus tests may use it. Check test files too. Also check suites that construct walker commands (readme-quickstart-suite.ts). And `AspireCommandRunner` used in quickstart-walk gates? grep found only aspire-walk + readme-command in non-test files. Check tests: quickstart tests inject run seam — with 3-arg signature `(command, cwd, timeoutMs)` — assignable to 4-param type? A function with fewer params is assignable to a type with more params in TS. Fine.\n\nItem 9: residual collision ways. Deno 2.9.5: `deno install --global --name netscript` — where does it install? DENO_INSTALL_ROOT/bin if set; also `deno install` checks for existing installation... The error \"Existing installation found\" occurs when the binary already exists at the target location. With DENO_INSTALL_ROOT set to the run-owned root (freshly created empty), `deno install` writes `<root>/bin/netscript`. Also note: does `deno install` respect DENO_INSTALL_ROOT? Yes — deno respects DENO_INSTALL_ROOT for global installs (docs). But one nuance: Deno uses DENO_INSTALL_ROOT, falling back to $DENO_DIR? Actually per Deno docs: \"deno install --global ... installs to $DENO_INSTALL_ROOT/bin, defaulting to $DENO_HOME/bin or $HOME/.deno/bin\". Setting DENO_INSTALL_ROOT env var is the documented mechanism. So relies only on documented behavior. Residual: the PATH prepend means `netscript` resolves to the isolated bin; later `deno`/`aspire`/`curl` resolve from ambient tail (prepend, not replace). One residual: `netscript init` etc. invoke... netscript may internally shell out to `deno` — resolves to ambient deno (fine). Another residual: