{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen-1788402623-b9feDHfHchsfaJzFWwII","type":"message","role":"assistant","container":null,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent — `deno install --help` documents the shim-location resolution order: `--root` option → `DENO_INSTALL_ROOT` env var → `$HOME/.deno`. The README command uses no `--root` flag, so DENO_INSTALL_ROOT governs. That's exactly the documented mechanism the implementation relies on. And `-f, --force` exists but is not used.\n\nWait — one caveat on precedence: the help shows the order `--root option`, `DENO_INSTALL_ROOT environment variable`, `$HOME/.deno`. So with no `--root`, DENO_INSTALL_ROOT wins over $HOME/.deno. The workflow's pre-existing install lives in the runner's default root ($HOME/.deno/bin or wherever the earlier step installed) — since the README command sets DENO_INSTALL_ROOT to the fresh run-owned root, the \"Existing installation found\" check is evaluated against the fresh root, which is empty → install succeeds. Confirmed documented behavior, nothing beyond it.\n\nHmm, one more nuance for item 9: DENO_INSTALL_ROOT points at a directory whose `bin` subdirectory holds shims (Deno appends `bin`). The implementation sets DENO_INSTALL_ROOT = `<runRoot>/.deno-install` and PATH prepend = `<runRoot>/.deno-install/bin` — consistent with Deno appending `/bin`. And they pre-create the root (deno install creates bin itself). Good.\n\nAlso the residual collision path: could `deno install` resolve the existing installation via PATH entry? No — the check is on the shim path under the resolved root, not PATH. And PATH override handles later `netscript` resolution.\n\nOne residual worth flagging as a note: `--env-file` behavior mentioned in help (\"Existing process environment variables are not overwritten\") — irrelevant (no env-file).\n\nNow item 8 residual: readState rejecting prior-run state lacking denoInstallRoot — judged fail-closed and correct (state.json only written by index ≥1 after a successful index 0 in the same PR run; a pre-fix stale state.json is ignored at index 0 and overwritten on success).\n\nWait — actually, double-check one edge: at index 0, initializeState does NOT remove the stale `state.json` itself, only `.deno-install` and `receipts/`. If the index-0 command fails, the stale pre-fix state.json remains with nextIndex pointing somewhere — but the gate has already failed at index 0, so the suite aborts. No out-of-order execution risk because each gate invocation is dispatched in order by the suite and a failure aborts. Fine — and even on a re-run of index 0, initializeState ignores state.json. Good.\n\nOne more potential subtlety in the hosted flow: are the readme.quickstart child commands each run as separate `deno run` processes with the state file at `.llm/tmp/readme-quickstart/state.json` under repoRoot (shared across indexes)? Yes — suite passes the same statePath for all indexes. So index ≥1 read the persisted denoInstallRoot. Confirmed by suite code (statePath constant). Good.\n\nAlso item 2's \"PATH=<root>/bin<delimiter><ambient PATH>\" — implemented at line 197-199 using DELIMITER from @std/path (platform-appropriate). Note: on Linux `:`. Good.\n\nNow, what about `env` being `Record<string,string>` passed to Deno.Command — TS type of Deno.Command env option accepts `Record<string,string>` — fine, check gate passed.\n\nFindings to report (non-blocking notes):\n1. The GREEN commit also extends the test with the Addendum's seeded-binary/removal + receipt-environment assertions, so those specific assertions were never exercised RED against the unfixed tree. I compensated with a mutation run (removal block deleted → test fails at \"Expected function to reject\"). Non-blocking: RED/GREEN discipline technically holds for the isolation assertions (b1aafaaa6 fails), and the added assertions were mutation-verified.\n2. `--unstable-kv` flag: the task's literal check command is rejected by the run-deno-check wrapper (it already appends